#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# lookup

#% text
# `lookup(a, k)` gives the value of key `k` in the association `a`.

#% text
# `lookup(a, k, d)` gives `d` when `a` has no key `k`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Without a default, a missing key gives `failure(missing_key, k)`.

#% item
# `a[k]` is `lookup(a, k)`.

#% item
# Keys are compared by identity: `1` and `1.0` are different keys.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
a = assoc("H" -> 1.008, "He" -> 4.003, "Li" -> 6.94)
lookup(a, "He")
#% output
#= 4.003

#% cell
lookup(a, "Ne", missing("unknown"))
#% output
#= missing("unknown")

#% cell
lookup(a, "Ne")
#% output
#! failure(missing_key, "Ne")

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Keys of any kind:

#% cell
lookup(assoc([0, 0] -> "origin"), [0, 0])
#% output
#= "origin"

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# Known values from a table, computed values for the rest:

#% cell
special = assoc(0 -> 1, 1 -> 1)
fn g(k) = if key_exists(special, k) then special[k] else k*g(k - 1)
g(5)
#% output
#= 120

#% text
# Translating codes to names:

#% cell
names = assoc("n" -> "neutron", "p" -> "proton")
map(c => lookup(names, c, "?"), ["p", "n", "x"])
#% output
#= ["proton", "neutron", "?"]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# `key_exists` tests for a key without reading it:

#% cell
[key_exists(a, "Li"), key_exists(a, "Ne")]
#% output
#= [true, false]

#% text
# Indexing gives the same as `lookup`:

#% cell
a["Li"] === lookup(a, "Li")
#% output
#= true

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# An exact and a machine number are different keys:

#% cell
lookup(assoc(1 -> "one"), 1.0, "not found")
#% output
#= "not found"

#% text
# The default is evaluated before the lookup, even when the key exists; a default that recurses never ends, so test with `key_exists` instead (as above):

#% cell
lookup(assoc(1 -> 2), 1, print("default evaluated"))
#% output
#= 2

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `assoc`, `key_exists`, `part`, `keys`, `values`
